{"type":"standard","title":"Absolute value","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Absolute value</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q120812","titles":{"canonical":"Absolute_value","normalized":"Absolute value","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Absolute value</span></span>"},"pageid":991,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Absolute_value.svg/330px-Absolute_value.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":220},"originalimage":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Absolute_value.svg/960px-Absolute_value.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":600,"height":400},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1371476443","tid":"39046e3b-a176-11f1-80b0-5038cc8a3a2e","timestamp":"2026-08-26T17:47:36Z","description":"Distance from zero to a number","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_value","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_value?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_value?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Absolute_value"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_value","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Absolute_value","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Absolute_value?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Absolute_value"}},"extract":"In mathematics, the absolute value or modulus of a real number , denoted , is the (non-negative) magnitude of  measured without regard to its sign. Namely,  if  is a positive number, and  if  is negative, and . For example, the absolute value of 3 is 3, and the absolute value of −3 is also 3. The absolute value of a number may be thought of as its distance from zero.","extract_html":"<p>In mathematics, the <b>absolute value</b> or <b>modulus</b> of a real number <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87f9e315fd7e2ba406057a97300593c4802b53e4\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.33ex;height:1.676ex\" /></span>, <span class=\"nowrap\">denoted <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/4eb41e5fd5dc37eaa1718dfbf4bc082edb991936\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.838ex;width:2.623ex;height:2.843ex\" /></span>,</span> is the (non-negative) magnitude <span class=\"nowrap\">of <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87f9e315fd7e2ba406057a97300593c4802b53e4\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.33ex;height:1.676ex\" /></span></span> measured without regard to its sign. Namely, <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/55d2c1e557cc3eedc06f8cc736efca5add7066dc\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.838ex;width:7.052ex;height:2.843ex\" /></span> if <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87f9e315fd7e2ba406057a97300593c4802b53e4\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.33ex;height:1.676ex\" /></span> is a positive number, and <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/42dcc4b88f38f60c8a96a5b2f8d76f5e4ed8ea2c\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.838ex;width:8.86ex;height:2.843ex\" /></span> if <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/87f9e315fd7e2ba406057a97300593c4802b53e4\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.33ex;height:1.676ex\" /></span> is negative, and <span class=\"nowrap\"><span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/550214d956a5bde2719eccd73a8e90cb72a4f1fd\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.838ex;width:6.717ex;height:2.843ex\" /></span>.</span> For example, the absolute value of 3 <span class=\"nowrap\">is 3,</span> and the absolute value of −3 is <span class=\"nowrap\">also 3.</span> The absolute value of a number may be thought of as its distance from zero.</p>"}